+# Privacy and third-party review
+
+## Scope and data minimization
+
+This application serves static coffee shop content. It has no implemented account,
+checkout, rewards, or contact workflows. Navigation labels do not imply those
+services exist. The only added route is `/privacy-policy`.
+
+The server processes the URL and connection information in memory to deliver a
+response. It does not log requests, read submitted bodies, write visitor data to
+disk, set cookies, use browser storage, or run JavaScript. Query parameters are
+ignored and never reflected. Only GET and HEAD are accepted. The server exposes an
+explicit allowlist of public files; repository files cannot be served.
+
+There is no personal-data database, backup, retention job, export, or deletion
+endpoint because the application stores no visitor records. Do not add dummy
+privacy controls that imply records exist. No consent banner is needed for the
+current implementation because no optional tracking is present.
+
+## Third-party inventory and audit
+
+Implementation review date: 2026-10-20. This is a repository review, not an
+independent audit or compliance certification.
+
+| Component | Finding and disposition | Visitor data shared by application |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Unsplash hero photograph | Replaced hotlink with local `public/assets/coffee.jpg`; source below. No runtime vendor request. | None |
+| `pnpm dlx statikk` | Removed executable package download from startup. Replaced with Node standard-library server. | None |
+| npm packages | No direct or transitive third-party dependencies. Committed lockfile; `npm audit --omit=dev` reports zero vulnerabilities. | None |
+| Fonts, analytics, ads, embeds, payment processors | None included. External connections and scripts blocked by CSP. | None |
+| Node runtime | Supplied by deployment environment. Keep on a supported, patched Node 24 release. Package audit does not audit the runtime. | Connection metadata is transiently processed |
+| Hosting, TLS terminator, CDN, DNS | Deployment provider unknown; external review remains required before launch. | Infrastructure may process IP addresses and connection metadata |
+
+Photograph source:
+https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1350&q=80
+
+Before introducing a vendor, record the owner, purpose, exact fields shared,
+necessity, retention period, deletion process, access controls, subprocessors,
+hosting locations, contractual protections, and review date. Prefer a local asset
+or no collection. New optional analytics must remain disabled until an explicit
+choice is made; honor withdrawal and applicable browser privacy preferences.
+Review the inventory on every integration change and at least quarterly. Arrange
+an independent audit when the production environment and any data workflows are
+available; none has been performed by this change.
+
+## Secure handling and deployment
+
+Run `npm start` (Node 24+) to listen on `PORT`, default 11222. Do not serve the
+`public` directory with a generic server that omits these headers.
+
+- Terminate HTTPS at a trusted reverse proxy. Redirect HTTP to HTTPS there and set
+  `HTTPS_DEPLOYMENT=true` on the application to enable one-year HSTS. The server
+  itself speaks HTTP; do not expose its upstream port publicly. HSTS does not
+  configure certificates or encrypt that upstream connection.
+- Preserve application security headers on all proxy responses, including errors.
+  CSP denies scripts, connections, forms, framing, objects, and external assets;
+  only same-origin images and CSS are allowed. Inline styles have been removed.
+- `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer` prevents link referrer disclosure. `nosniff`,
+  `DENY`, browser permission restrictions, and same-origin resource/opener policies
+  restrict unnecessary capabilities. Responses use `Cache-Control: no-store`.
+- Disable or minimize host/CDN access logs, URL query logging, analytics, and
+  request-body capture. Document any necessary operational retention and deletion
+  period with the hosting operator and update the public privacy page accordingly.
+- Restrict infrastructure access, require MFA, patch Node and the OS, and protect
+  any operational backups. Do not add secrets to public files or URLs.
+- If future services collect personal information, define the minimum fields,
+  purpose, access rules, retention/deletion, encryption, and user rights before
+  enabling collection. Implement server-side authorization and validation then.
+
+Header references:
+[CSP](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy),
+[Referrer Policy](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Referrer-Policy),
+[Permissions Policy](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Permissions-Policy).
+
+## Verification
+
+`npm test` checks headers on pages, assets, errors, and unsupported methods; HEAD
+behavior; blocked repository access; no cookies or query reflection; explicit HSTS
+configuration; and absence of external resources, forms, and inline code.
+`npm audit --omit=dev` checks package advisories. No manual browser verification
+was performed. Production TLS, proxy headers, host logging/retention, and an
+independent assessment must be verified in the actual deployment.
